sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch08-23> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because regions of tissue a few tenths of a millimeter across subdivide into regions using different sections of the genetic program (controlled by chemical gradients and homeobox genes), embryos necessarily develop hierarchically, e.g. a leg region before a foot region.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Animal embryos develop hierarchically because of a fixed length scale";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 8, “Implications for Everyday Systems”; section “Growth of Plants and Animals” (from p. 400), p. 419. Scope: embryonic development of animals"
}
